n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=b\\) meets the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the largest possible value of \\(n+1-b\\) is \\(n\\).\nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=a\\) and for a diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\nUsing Lemma 1 and Lemma 2, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k=n-1\\) the right–hand side of (2) equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2- \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k=n\\) it equals  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth bounds are strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\) given by (1); therefore a\nconfiguration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot satisfy the covering condition.\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* **Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines**  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad\\text{for }i=1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-k .\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)**  \n\n  \\[\n  L_j:\\;y=x+j-1\\qquad\\text{for }j=1,2,\\dots ,\\,k .\n  \\]\n\nEach \\(H_i\\) belongs to the family \\(\\mathcal H\\) and is therefore non‑sunny;\neach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is sunny.\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\)*, set \\(t=b-a\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt=b-a\\le n+1-2a\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) is an integer with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\), and the point satisfies\n\\(b=a+t\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_{t+1}\\) (since \\(L_{t+1}\\) is defined by\n\\(y=x+t\\)).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nConsequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen\nlines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth the impossibility for \\(k=n-1,n\\) and the explicit construction for all\n\\(k\\le n-2\\) have been proved rigorously.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
