ines satisfying the two required conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) define the *antidiagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad |D_c|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Inductive sunny covering)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_m\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  Choose any line through \\((1,1)\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and clearly covers the single point.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge 1\\); let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a family of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_m\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_{m+1}\\setminus S_m\\) consists exactly of the antidiagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+2}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=m+2\\},\n\\qquad |D_{m+2}|=m+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the direction \\((1,-1)\\) of the antidiagonals, each line \\(L_i\\) meets \\(D_{m+2}\\) in **at most one** point.  Consequently at most \\(m\\) points of \\(D_{m+2}\\) are already covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\); at least one point \\(P\\in D_{m+2}\\) remains uncovered.\n\nThrough \\(P\\) pass infinitely many lines.  Only three slopes are forbidden \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and the slopes of the already existing lines \\(\\{\\,\\text{slope}(L_i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\) are also forbidden if we want a new line to be distinct.  Since the set of forbidden slopes is finite, we can choose a slope different from all of them; the resulting line \\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny, distinct from the previous ones, and passes through the uncovered point \\(P\\).\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(S_{m+1}=S_m\\cup D_{m+2}\\).  This completes the induction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Horizontal covering of the lower part)\n\nFor any integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nH:=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By definition a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on the unique horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belonging to \\(H\\); conversely every point of a line \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) obviously satisfies \\(b=j\\le n-k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covering all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n2. **Translation of the remaining points.**  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) that are *not* covered by the horizontals of Lemma\u202f2 are those with \\(b>n-k\\).  \n   For such a point write \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\) (so \\(b'\\ge 1\\)).  \n   The map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,b')\\) sends the set  \n\n   \\[\n   R:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b>n-k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   bijectively onto \\(S_k\\).  Hence \\(R\\) is a translated copy of \\(S_k\\).\n\n3. **Covering the translated copy.**  \n   Translate each sunny line \\(S_i\\) by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); denote the translated line by \\(\\widetilde S_i\\).  \n   Because translation preserves parallelism, each \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) is still sunny and distinct from the others.  \n   Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in R\\) we have \\((a,b')\\in S_k\\) and therefore \\((a,b')\\) lies on some \\(S_i\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding \\(\\widetilde S_i\\).  Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k:=\\{\\widetilde S_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   covers all points of \\(R\\).\n\n4. **Complete family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k\\;\\cup\\;H ,\n   \\qquad H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k\\) are sunny; the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines are not sunny.  \n   By construction the union of the two subfamilies equals \\(S_n\\); therefore every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, any admissible configuration obviously contains at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of solution.**"

