isting lines \\(\\{\\,\\operatorname{slope}(L_i)\\mid1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\).  Pick a slope different from all of them; the resulting line \\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny, distinct from the previous ones, and passes through \\(P\\).  \n     Then \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{m+1}=S_m\\cup D_{m+2}\\). \u220e  \n\n   Combining Lemma\u202f0 and Lemma\u202f1 we have:\n\n   \\[\n   (\\star_m):\\qquad\\text{\u201cthere exist }m\\text{ distinct sunny lines covering }S_m\\text{\u201d}\n   \\]\n   holds for every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (Horizontal covering of the lower part).**  \n   For any integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family  \n\n   \\[\n   H:=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines and covers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n   *Proof.*  If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(y=b\\in H\\); conversely every point on a line \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) clearly satisfies \\(b=j\\le n-k\\). \u220e  \n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  By \\((\\star_k)\\) obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covering \\(S_k\\).\n\n   *Translation of the remaining points.*  The points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by the horizontals of Lemma\u202f2 are those with \\(b>n-k\\).  Define a translation vector \\(\\mathbf v=(0,n-k)\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde S_i:=S_i+\\mathbf v\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\]\n\n   Because translation preserves parallelism, each \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) is still sunny and the family \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k:=\\{\\widetilde S_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde S_k\\}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage of the translated part.*  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>n