isting lines \\(\\{\\,\\operatorname{slope}(L_i)\\mid1\\le i\\le m\\,\\}\\).  Pick a slope different from all of them; the resulting line \\(L_{m+1}\\) is sunny, distinct from the previous ones, and passes through \\(P\\).  \n     Then \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{L_{m+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{m+1}=S_m\\cup D_{m+2}\\). \u220e  \n\n   Combining Lemma\u202f0 and Lemma\u202f1 we have:\n\n   \\[\n   (\\star_m):\\qquad\\text{\u201cthere exist }m\\text{ distinct sunny lines covering }S_m\\text{\u201d}\n   \\]\n   holds for every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\).\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (Horizontal covering of the lower part).**  \n   For any integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family  \n\n   \\[\n   H:=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines and covers precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n   *Proof.*  If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(y=b\\in H\\); conversely every point on a line \\(y=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) clearly satisfies \\(b=j\\le n-k\\). \u220e  \n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n\n   *Sunny part.*  By \\((\\star_k)\\) obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covering \\(S_k\\).\n\n   *Translation of the remaining points.*  The points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by the horizontals of Lemma\u202f2 are those with \\(b>n-k\\).  Define a translation vector \\(\\mathbf v=(0,n-k)\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde S_i:=S_i+\\mathbf v\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\]\n\n   Because translation preserves parallelism, each \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) is still sunny and the family \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k:=\\{\\widetilde S_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde S_k\\}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage of the translated part.*  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>n-k\\) write \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\).  Then \\(b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b'\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1\\); hence \\((a,b')\\in S_k\\).  By definition of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) there exists an index \\(i\\) with \\((a,b')\\in S_i\\); adding the vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) shows \\((a,b)\\in\\widetilde S_i\\).  Thus every point with \\(b>n-k\\) lies on some \\(\\widetilde S_i\\).\n\n   *Complete family.*  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k\\;\\cup\\;H .\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines.  \n   - The \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal S}_k\\) are sunny.  \n   - The \\(n-k\\) lines of \\(H\\) are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so they are pairwise distinct.  \n   The original sunny lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct; translating by the same vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) preserves distinctness, so the \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) are also pairwise distinct.  \n   Moreover, a sunny line is never horizontal, therefore no \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) can coincide with any line of \\(H\\).  Hence all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Full coverage verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   - If \\(b\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in H\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   - If \\(b>n-k\\), then as shown above \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(\\widetilde S_i\\).  \n\n   In either case the point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the required covering condition.\n\n6. **Necessity of the bound \\(k\\le n\\).**  \n   Any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above yields \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Conversely, no configuration can have \\(k>n\\).  Therefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]"
